     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That's about twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg per day (some references allow up to 3,000 mg). A single 1700 mg dose is also well above the usual maximum of 1,000 mg or less per dose. A dose this high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **A licensed prescriber needs to place it.** You've said you aren't a clinician. Medication orders should come from the patient's treating prescriber, or be verified by one.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- If the dose is a typo, the treating prescriber can confirm the intended dose. For example, 850 mg three times daily with meals is 2,550 mg per day, and 500 mg or 1000 mg twice daily are also common regimens.\n- If the dose is intended, the prescriber should confirm it directly and document the rationale. A pharmacist should review it too.\n\nOnce an authorized prescriber gives me a verified order, I can enter it right away."
      }
     ],
